 ],
 "literal": "Restrained tears broken, red-streaked cool eyes, / with an unbearable affliction, distressed by loneliness, / are there others also who hear? Raining heavily, / with the cold wind scattering, in the midnight of the cold season, / whenever the hearts of the cows grieve, bellowing, / the pitiful sound of the curved bell with its moving tongue!",
 "translation": "My cool, red-streaked eyes have broken their restraint of tears. Distressed by loneliness and an unbearable affliction, I wonder: are there others awake to hear this? In the midnight of the cold season, as the freezing wind scatters the heavy rain, the curved bells with moving tongues make a pitiful, bellowing sound whenever the hearts of the cows grieve.",
 "modern": "கண்ணீரைத் தடுத்து நிறுத்தும் வரம்பு உடைந்துபோன, சிவந்த வரிகளையுடைய குளிர்ந்த கண்களிலிருந்து நீர் வழிய, தாங்க முடியாத நோயோடும் தனிமையோடும் வருந்தி, நான் கலங்கி நிற்கிறேன். இதைப் பிறரும் கேட்கிறார்களா? மழை மிகுந்து பெய்ய, வாடைக் காற்று வீசும் இந்தக் குளிர்கால நள்ளிரவில், பசுக்களின் உள்ளங்கள் வருந்தும்போதெல்லாம் ஒலிக்கின்ற, நாக்கு அசையும் வளைந்த மணிகளின் மெல்லிய ஓசை கேட்கிறதே!",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the heroine, who is suffering in the absence of her lover during the cold season. She is awake at midnight, weeping, and wonders if there are others who can hear her grief or the sorrowful sounds of the night. The image of the cows grieving in the cold, and the bells around their necks making a pitiful sound as they move, mirrors her own internal state of loneliness and suffering. This poem belongs to the Kuriñci tiṇai, where the cold season and midnight intensify the feelings of separation and longing. Key words include 'kūthir' (cold season), 'yāmathu' (midnight), 'pulampal' (loneliness/grief), and 'koṭumaṇi' (curved bell).",
